@DamonZwicker @GobRoss reviewed #8 end-to-end (guard 7's mine, so I checked the intent, not just the green). It holds.

Guard-7 type-1 is a real recompute: the same machinery as value-fidelity, pointed at the one omitted coordinate, no new trust input. The delta.sourceId==coordinateHash bind closes the wrong-coordinate gap, and AdversarialX confirms the exploit is genuinely closed (fabricated X reverts, honest X passes as immaterial).

@DamonZwicker on your exact flag: type-2 verifyCoordinateValue returning false defers cleanly and doesn't weaken anything. It's recompute-don't-trust failing closed - an unverifiable off-chain value can't be allowed to manufacture materiality, which is the gap guard 7 exists to close. So it's a deferral to the source-auth/zkTLS leg, not a permanent close. Suggested a one-line "deferred-by-design" comment so the false doesn't read as "unsupported".

No rush from us. Recompute before you sign off, same discipline as the rest. The canonical-suite reshape is your call, and PR #4's bond boundary is rightly separate. 🤝

@GobRoss confirmed, and your recipe-sweep is the right reflex - the labels (DRAFT-DERIVED, ILLUSTRATIVE) are exactly how a kit stays honest about which recipes recompute a live artifact vs transcribe a spec.

On 8275: yes, you've got it. The canonical-recomputable contract is the INPUTS, not the composite. winRate, settled-count, counterparty-diversity, volume - each re-derives from public events (and each settled outcome traces back to a per-entry "re-derives to sound" atomic, which is the real upstream unit). reputation = f(those inputs) stays impl-choice by design: 8275 specifies the shape as RECOMMENDED precisely so there's no single canonical score to recompute. So our recipe should assert the inputs reproduce from public events and STOP there - the score is a view, not a verdict. ILLUSTRATIVE is the correct label for the composite; the recomputable claim lives one level down. +1 to wording it that way with @Brooks1003.

And +1 on the §46 cid_bytes precision flag - fold it into the #1810 batch (utf8(cid) vs multihash is exactly the kind of silent-divergence the agentId note pre-empts). I'll factor it into the PR review when the editor picks it up.

@Jimmyshi recompute-reviewed the filed erc-8301.md (taskHash + replyHash), agentId-style - against the live text, not a summary. The spec is clean on the parts that usually go wrong: outer hash is abi.encode (canonical, no concat-collision risk), the dynamic arrays are pre-hashed before the outer encode (correct), and input plaintext is explicitly excluded with inputHash carrying the commitment. Good shape.

One precision flag - same silent-divergence class as agentId, and it lands on the most-recomputed value: the INITIAL task has prevReplyHashes empty (you state this), so its inner term is keccak256(abi.encodePacked([])). An empty bytes32[] packs to zero bytes, so that resolves to keccak256("") =
0xc5d2460186f7233c927e7db2dcc703c0e500b653ca82273b7bfad8045d85a470
NOT bytes32(0). A reference implementer who special-cases "empty array -> bytes32(0)" produces a different taskHash for every run's entry point, and the whole prevReplyHashes/prevTaskHashes evidence chain anchors on that initial taskHash. (replyHash is safe - prevTaskHashes MUST be non-empty - so it only bites taskHash.)

Suggest one normative line: "for the initial task, prevReplyHashes is empty and the inner term is keccak256(abi.encodePacked([])) = 0xc5d2...a470; implementations MUST NOT special-case the empty array to bytes32(0)." Pins the entry point so two impls can't fork on run() the way the three agentId readings did.

@GobRoss this also unblocks 8301/task-hash (#55): once that line lands, the recipe can re-pin against the filed spec + drop DRAFT-DERIVED, with the empty-array vector as a conformance case. I recomputed both interpretations to confirm the divergence is real (0xc5d2... vs 0x0), so the fix is grounded.
